+### 2026-09-05 00:20Z — the r11l eval COMPLETED and reached L2. Not attributed to anything.
+
+The operator-authorized eval finished at 23:46Z after 8,590 s (2 h 23 m) and wrote
+`results/arc_leaderboard_eval_runs/r11l-3114878.json`. It did not die. The dashboard reported
+`gone; no receipt was armed, so the exit is UNKNOWN`, which was correct and was my omission, not
+a fault of the run — see the receipt note below.
+
+Same seed, same budget, same policy as the control:
+
+| | control `r11l-2491317` | this run `r11l-3114878` |
+|---|---|---|
+| policy / budget / seed | e3 / 20000 / 20260719 | e3 / 20000 / 20260719 |
+| levels reached | 1 | **2** |
+| total actions | 2,077 | 2,121 |
+| actions to first level-up | 815 | 813 |
+| level 1 | 1,262 actions, NOT completed | **123 actions, completed** |
+| generator completions | 13 | 5 |
+| `chars_final` | 37,872 | 20,434 |
+| wall clock | about 6 h 57 m | 2 h 23 m |
+
+**Where the difference is.** Level 0 is effectively identical, 813 actions against 815. The
+whole difference sits at level 1: the control spent 1,262 actions there and never finished it;
+this run finished it in 123. It also used fewer generator calls and a third of the wall clock.
+
+**This is NOT evidence that anything was fixed, and must not be cited as such.** It is one
+sample against one sample. The seed fixes the environment, not the generator, which samples; two
+runs of a stochastic policy are two draws, not an A/B. Checked for a confound rather than
+assumed: exactly two commits touched
+`arc_competition_agent.py` / `arc_executable_world_model.py` / `arc_leaderboard_eval.py`
+between the two runs, and neither changes runtime behaviour — one is a conductor checkpoint and
+one is a commit-time lint (REQ-ARC-WMTE-6642/6643). So no deliberate solver change separates
+them. Run-to-run generator variance is the leading explanation and the data cannot exclude it.
+
+What the run does establish, plainly: **the live agent reached r11l level 2 by frame-only
+self-discovery, with no GameAdapter and no banked plan.** The registry's live self-discovery
+count moved from 4 levels to 5.
+
+**The `efficiency` field is NOT quoted here on purpose.** It reads 0.0035 for the control and
+0.689 for this run, and neither value reproduces from the per-level formula the file documents.
+`scripts/arc_leaderboard_eval.py:793` says in its own comment that the doc paraphrase "was WRONG
+on three counts". Levels and action counts are unambiguous; the efficiency scalar needs its
+definition settled before anyone cites it, and this session did not settle it.
+
+**An orphaned llama-server held both GPUs for about 26 minutes after the run exited, on the
+conductor's port.** PID 3115288, reparented to `ppid 1`, 10,554 MiB on GPU 0 and 11,376 MiB on
+GPU 1, both cards at 0 percent utilisation, **listening on 127.0.0.1:8919 — the conductor's E3
+default port**, where `LocalGGUFProposer` adopts an existing server without checking the model.
+The conductor's next E3 task would have silently used a 27B at `n_ctx=98304` across both cards
+believing it started its own.
+
+Reaped correctly rather than reflexively: `ss -tnp state established` showed **zero** clients
+first, because `ppid 1` is not by itself abandonment. SIGTERM to the exact PID, never
+`pkill -f`. It exited in about two seconds and both cards returned to 4 MiB. The 83.9 percent
+CPU it showed was a lifetime average; 0 percent GPU utilisation was the honest signal.
+
+**Process lesson, recorded in memory.** A long run must arm a receipt (REQ-INFRA-6830,
+`python/carnot/testing/long_run_receipt.py`). This one was launched with a bare `nohup`, so a
+clean success presented as an unexplained disappearance for a full hour of checking. The run's
+own log carried the answer the whole time.
+
